<h1>Asset groups &amp; streaming</h1>
<p>An <strong>asset group</strong> is a named set of assets and scenes prepared together, with memory limits and an upload time target. Use groups to manage level residency and monitor preparation before activating a level.</p>

<p>Headless and serve runs verify visual sources and resource ownership without allocating GPU resources or retaining unused encoded image/model copies. Text and data resources retain their normal loading behavior. <code>Assets.text</code> reads resident text/data sources, not visual resources.</p>
<h2>What a group is</h2>
<p>A group is an authored <code>*.asset-group.json</code> document listing <strong>roots</strong> (what to load) and a <strong>budget</strong> (memory and per-frame upload targets). Roots are <code>asset://</code> or <code>scene://</code> URIs. Loading a <code>scene://</code> root pulls in the assets referenced by that scene, its HUD, and expanded prefabs.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "id": "asset-group://skyforge_outpost",
  "roots": [
    "asset://levels/skyforge_outpost",
    "scene://game/skyforge_outpost"
  ],
  "budget": {
    "resident_mb": 192,
    "decoded_mb": 384,
    "upload_ms_per_frame": 3
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>format_version</code> — required on every authored document.</li>
  <li><code>id</code> — stable group ID.</li>
  <li><code>roots</code> — array of <code>asset://</code> and <code>scene://</code> URIs to resolve and load as one batch.</li>
  <li><code>budget.resident_mb</code> — cap in megabytes on memory kept resident.</li>
  <li><code>budget.decoded_mb</code> — cap in megabytes on decoded (pre-upload) memory.</li>
  <li><code>budget.upload_ms_per_frame</code> — target milliseconds of upload work per update.</li>
</ul>
<p>The upload budget is a scheduling target, not a hard frame-time limit. Ordinary loaders yield between resources when the target is reached. Native backends that replace a resource registry atomically combine ready resources into one transaction; that transaction can exceed the target. Memory limits still apply to the group, and a failed native replacement preserves the previous resource snapshot. Inspect <code>Assets.memory_report()</code> and measure loading on the target platform before choosing budgets.</p>
<p>Memory counters describe storage retained by each resource loader. Native registry loaders retain source verification records rather than complete encoded files, and report source-file sizes as a residency estimate. Renderer decoding buffers and GPU allocations have separate lifetimes; <code>decoded_mb</code> is not a hard cap on all renderer transient or GPU memory.</p>

<h2>Startup preload versus on-demand</h2>
<p>The project document's <code>assets</code> array is <strong>startup preload only</strong> — the few things needed before the first frame (the title font, a placeholder). It does not load every project asset automatically. Everything else loads when a group asks for it, when its scene loads, or when script code calls <code>Assets.load</code>.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "name": "skyforge",
  "assets": [
    "asset://fonts/skyforge_ui",
    "asset://textures/skyforge_placeholder"
  ]
}</code></pre>
<p>Only these two load at startup. A weapon texture referenced by a prefab loads when that prefab's scene loads. Keeping the startup array tiny is how Skyforge reaches its first frame quickly.</p>

<h2>Scene transitions</h2>
<p>A scene transition prepares an implicit group from its <code>scene://</code> root. The authored scene, HUD, expanded prefabs, and their asset dependencies become ready before activation at a frame boundary. A failed preparation leaves the active scene in place. An authored group is optional; scene references do not need to be repeated in a startup group.</p>
<pre><code>local Scene = require("demi.scene")

Scene.prepare("scene://game/skyforge_outpost")  -- prepares the scene and its referenced assets

function Game:on_update()
    if Scene.is_prepared() then
        Scene.activate()   -- swap only when every root is ready
    end
end</code></pre>
<p><code>Scene.prepare</code> / <code>Scene.is_prepared</code> / <code>Scene.activate</code> follow the two-phase pattern from <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'scene-and-prefab-api'}) }}">Scene &amp; prefab API</a>. The scene keeps its acquired assets until it is unloaded; shared assets stay resident while another owner still uses them.</p>

<h2>Loading a group from script</h2>
<p>The same <code>demi.assets</code> API loads an individual <code>asset://</code> resource or an <code>asset-group://</code> batch. Keep the returned request ID while waiting or cancelling.</p>
<pre><code>local Assets = require("demi.assets")
local Game = {}

local request, err = Assets.load("asset-group://skyforge_outpost")
if not request or request == 0 then error(err) end

function Game:on_update()
    if not Assets.is_ready(request) then
        local p = Assets.progress(request)
        -- p.stage and p.fraction describe this request
    end
end

return Game</code></pre>
<p><code>Assets.load(uri)</code> returns a request ID and an error string. <code>Assets.is_ready(request)</code> returns a boolean. <code>Assets.progress(request)</code> returns a table with <code>stage</code>, <code>fraction</code>, byte counts, and any error. Call <code>Assets.unload(uri)</code> when this owner no longer needs the group. The full load API is in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'assets-time-api'}) }}">Assets, time &amp; timers</a>.</p>
<p>During staged native preparation, <code>fraction</code> includes completed CPU preparation and GPU upload units within this request's pending batch. It measures completed work units rather than elapsed time, so differently sized assets can advance at different rates. The fraction reaches <code>1</code> only after the prepared resources have been published and the request is ready.</p>

